tel 1.0

base hieroglyph
import soundness

info   character encodings, sanitisers, display widths, Unicode properties, grapheme breaks and collation
detail
    _Hieroglyph_ is where text meets bytes and where a `Char` meets Unicode. The boundary
    between `Text` and `Data` is a character encoding, and the library makes that choice
    explicit and typed: a `Codepage` turns text into bytes, a `Charset` turns bytes back into
    text, and each is a contextual value chosen by importing one of the `codepages` or
    `charsets` givens by name, so no conversion happens under an assumed platform default.
    What to do with bytes which cannot be decoded is a separate, equally explicit choice: a
    `TextSanitizer` from the `textSanitizers` package, which skips them, substitutes `?`,
    raises `Charset.Error` at the first fault, or accrues every fault while decoding the rest.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import codepages.utf8Codepage
    import charsets.utf8Charset
    import textSanitizers.strictSanitizer
    import strategies.throwUnsafely

    val bytes: Data = t"café".in[Data]   // 5 bytes of UTF-8
    bytes.utf8                           // t"café"
    enc"ISO-8859-1".encoder.encoded(t"café").length   // 4
    ```

    Any encoding the JVM knows is named with the `enc"…"` interpolator, which checks as the
    code compiles that the encoding exists, and whether it can encode as well as decode:
    `enc"ISO-2022-CN".decoder` compiles, `enc"ISO-2022-CN".encoder` does not. Encoding and
    decoding also run as stream stages (`Codepage#encoded` and `Charset#decoded` over a
    `Chain`), and a multi-byte character or a surrogate pair split across two chunks is
    carried over the boundary rather than corrupted.

    Alongside the encodings are the character-level facts a program sometimes needs:

    - **display width** — `metrics` measures a `Char` or a `Text` through the `Measurable`
      given in scope, chosen from `textMetrics`: `uniformMetric` counts every character as one
      column, `eastAsianScriptsMetric` gives wide ideographs two, and
      `wideCharacterWidthMetric` additionally gives combining marks and controls none;
    - **Unicode properties** — the extensions `letter`, `digit`, `whitespace`, `control`,
      `printable`, `majuscular`, `minuscular`, `description`, `superscript` and `subscript` on
      `Char`, the `ucs"…"` interpolator for a character by its Unicode name, and `Unicode`'s
      tables of names and east-Asian widths;
    - **grapheme clusters** — `GraphemeBreak.boundaries` finds where one user-perceived
      character ends and the next begins, by the rules of UAX #29;
    - **normalisation and collation** — `Normalization.decompose` gives a text's canonical
      decomposition (NFD), and `CollationTable` orders text by the Unicode Collation Algorithm
      (UTS #10), with `tailor` for the common European tailorings. These are what
      gossamer's `collations.unicodeCollation` and `Writing` are built on. The collation types
      are reached as `hieroglyph.CollationTable`, `hieroglyph.CollationRule` and
      `hieroglyph.CollationLevel`, which the `soundness` umbrella does not export.

    The library is one source set, `hieroglyph.core`, which compiles for the JVM and Scala
    Native. The Unicode data it needs (`UnicodeData.txt`, `EastAsianWidth.txt`,
    `GraphemeBreakProperty.txt`, `emoji-data.txt`, `IndicConjunctBreak.txt` and `allkeys.txt`,
    all version 16.0.0) is read from the classpath under `/hieroglyph/` when first used; on the
    JVM, a table missing from the classpath is fetched from unicode.org, while on Scala Native
    the tables must be embedded in the binary and a missing one is simply absent.

    Everything is exported to the `soundness` umbrella except the collation types, and the
    five choice packages — `charsets`, `codepages`, `textSanitizers`, `textMetrics` and
    `communication` — merge with the umbrella's own packages of those names. Since
    `import soundness.*` imports no givens, a program names the encoding, sanitiser and metric
    it wants: `import codepages.utf8Codepage`, `import textSanitizers.strictSanitizer`,
    `import textMetrics.uniformMetric`. The `iso88591Charset` and `iso88591Codepage` givens are
    declared only in `hieroglyph.charsets` and `hieroglyph.codepages`, not re-exported in the
    umbrella's packages.

refer hieroglyph.Charset
refer hieroglyph.Codepage
refer hieroglyph.Encoding
refer hieroglyph.TextSanitizer
refer hieroglyph.Measurable
refer hieroglyph.GraphemeBreak

entry .Encoding   a named character encoding the platform supports, from which a `Charset` or a `Codepage` is made
  detail
      An `Encoding` is a character encoding by name — `UTF-8`, `ISO-8859-1`, `ISO-2022-CN` —
      and the common root of the two directions of conversion: `#decoder` makes the `Charset`
      which reads bytes as text, and `.encoder` makes the `Codepage` which writes text as
      bytes. Its type member `#CanEncode` records, as `true` or `false`, whether the platform
      can encode with it, and `.encoder` exists only on an `Encoding { type CanEncode = true }`,
      so an encoding which can only decode has no way to be asked for bytes.

      The way to get one is the `enc"…"` interpolator, which checks the name as the code
      compiles and gives the precise `CanEncode` type:

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textSanitizers.skipSanitizer

      enc"UTF-8".encoder          // a Codepage
      enc"ISO-2022-CN".decoder    // a Charset
      enc"ISO-2022-CN".encoder    // does not compile
      enc"ABCDEF"                 // does not compile
      ```

      At runtime, `Encoding.unapply(name)` looks a name up in the platform's table (case
      insensitively, by display name or alias) as an `Option`, with `CanEncode` left abstract.
      `#name` is the platform's canonical display name, whatever spelling the encoding was
      asked for by, and `#charset` is the `java.nio.charset.Charset` behind it, resolved once.

      An encoding renders as text as its name (`.textualizable`), and as a message the same
      way (`.communicable`); its `toString` is the source which would make it,
      `enc"UTF-8"`.
  refer hieroglyph.enc
  refer hieroglyph.Charset
  refer hieroglyph.Codepage
  entry .textualizable   renders an encoding as its canonical name
  entry .communicable   renders an encoding as a message holding its name
      The given which lets an `Encoding` be interpolated into an `m"…"` message, as
      `Charset.Error` and `Codepage.Error` do.
  entry .unapply   looks an encoding up by name at runtime, as an `Option`
      `Encoding.unapply(t"utf-8")` is `Some(encoding)` if the platform has an encoding of that
      name or alias, compared case-insensitively, and `None` otherwise. The result's
      `CanEncode` is not known statically, so `encoder` is unavailable on it; for a name known
      when the code is written, `enc"…"` gives the precise type. It is an extractor, so it also
      serves in a match: `case Encoding(encoding) =>`.
  entry .apply   makes an encoding from a name and whether it can encode, without checking either
      `Encoding(name, canEncode)` wraps a name as an `Encoding { type CanEncode = canEncode.type }`
      and does not verify that the platform has it: `#charset` resolves the name lazily, and a
      name the platform lacks fails there with the JDK's `UnsupportedCharsetException`. Prefer
      `enc"…"` or `.unapply`, which check.
  entry .encoder   the `Codepage` which encodes text as bytes in this encoding
      An extension available only on an `Encoding { type CanEncode = true }`, so it does not
      compile on a decode-only encoding such as `enc"ISO-2022-CN"`. `enc"UTF-8".encoder` is
      the same codepage `codepages.utf8Codepage` provides.
  entry #name   the encoding's canonical display name, as the platform spells it
  entry #CanEncode   whether the platform can encode with this encoding: `true`, `false`, or unknown
      A type member bounded by `Boolean`. `enc"…"` fixes it to `true` or `false` from the
      platform's table; `Encoding.unapply` leaves it abstract. Only an encoding whose
      `CanEncode` is `true` has `encoder`.
  entry #decoder   the `Charset` which decodes bytes of this encoding as text
      Needs a `TextSanitizer` in scope, which the charset keeps as its own and consults for
      every undecodable byte sequence. Every encoding can decode, so this is available
      whatever `CanEncode` is.
  entry #charset   the `java.nio.charset.Charset` behind this encoding, resolved once
      A lazy value, so the platform's lookup by name happens on first use and never again:
      `Codepage#encoded` passes this to `getBytes` rather than the name, since a lookup by name
      on every call is measurable in a byte-heavy pipeline.

entry .Charset   decodes bytes as text in one encoding, treating bad input as its sanitiser says
  detail
      A `Charset` is the decoding direction of an `Encoding`: `#decoded` turns `Data` into
      `Text`, either all at once or as a stream of chunks. It is made from an encoding with
      `Encoding#decoder` or found by name with `Charset.unapply`, and most often it is a given
      imported by name from the `charsets` package — `charsets.utf8Charset` and the others —
      which is what turbulence's `read[Text]`, `utf8` and the `Readable`/`Aggregable` givens
      for text over bytes resolve.

      Every charset carries a `TextSanitizer`, taken as a contextual value when it is made
      (`#sanitizer`), which decides what happens at a byte sequence the encoding cannot decode:
      `textSanitizers.skipSanitizer` drops it, `substituteSanitizer` writes `?`,
      `strictSanitizer` raises `Charset.Error` naming its position, and `accrueSanitizer`
      writes `?` and records a `Charset.Error` at each fault so that the whole input is
      recovered and every fault reported.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      val badUtf8 = Data(45, -62, 49, 48)   // `-`, then a truncated two-byte sequence, then `10`

      locally:
        import textSanitizers.skipSanitizer
        charsets.utf8Charset.decoded(badUtf8)   // t"-10"

      locally:
        import textSanitizers.substituteSanitizer
        charsets.utf8Charset.decoded(badUtf8)   // t"-?10"

      locally:
        import textSanitizers.strictSanitizer
        capture[Charset.Error](charsets.utf8Charset.decoded(badUtf8))   // Charset.Error(1, enc"UTF-8")
      ```

      Decoding a stream of chunks (`decoded(stream: Chain[Data])`) is incremental: a
      multi-byte character split across two chunks is carried over the boundary and decoded
      whole, and a sequence still incomplete when the input ends is a fault at the final
      position, so a truncated `café` under the strict sanitiser is `Charset.Error(4, …)`.

      A `Charset` is `Findable` with `Self = Text` and `Form = Data`, which is how it serves as
      the decoding duct in turbulence's stream stages.
  refer hieroglyph.Encoding
  refer hieroglyph.TextSanitizer
  refer hieroglyph.charsets
  refer hieroglyph.textSanitizers
  entry .system   the platform's default charset, with the sanitiser in scope
      `Charset.defaultCharset` wrapped as a `Charset`, which depends on the locale and the
      JVM's `file.encoding`, so it is not a given and is never resolved implicitly: a program
      which wants the platform default asks for it by this name. Needs a `TextSanitizer`.
  entry .unapply   finds a charset by encoding name at runtime, as an `Option`
      `Charset.unapply(t"ISO-8859-1")` is `Encoding.unapply(name)` wrapped as a `Charset`
      with the `TextSanitizer` in scope: `Some` if the platform has the encoding (by display
      name or alias, case insensitively), `None` otherwise. Usable as an extractor in a match.
      This is how the `charsets` givens are built, and how a charset named in a `Content-Type`
      header is resolved.
  entry .Focus   the position being decoded, for an accruing sanitiser's `validate` block
    detail
        A `Charset.Focus(position)` is what `textSanitizers.accrueSanitizer` puts in focus
        around each fault it reports, so that a `validate[Charset.Focus]` block, which
        accumulates the faults, learns where each occurred: `prior` in the block is the focus,
        an `Optional[Charset.Focus]`.

        ```scala
        import soundness.*
        import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
        import dysasymptotics.linearSize

        case class DecodeIssues(items: List[(Int, Charset.Error)] = Nil)(using Diagnostics)
        extends Error(m"${items.size} decoding issues"):
          def +(position: Int, error: Charset.Error): DecodeIssues =
            DecodeIssues(items :+ (position, error))

        val badUtf8 = Data(45, -62, 49, 48)

        validate[Charset.Focus](DecodeIssues()):
          case error: Charset.Error => accrual + (prior.let(_.position).or(0), error)
        . protect:
            import textSanitizers.accrueSanitizer
            charsets.utf8Charset.decoded(badUtf8)   // t"-?10", with one issue at position 1
        ```
    entry #position   the byte offset at which the fault occurred
  entry .Error   raised when a byte sequence cannot be decoded and the sanitiser is strict
    detail
        _The byte sequence at position `position` could not be decoded with the encoding
        `encoding`_. Raised through a `Tactic[Charset.Error]` by `textSanitizers.strictSanitizer`
        at the first undecodable sequence, and by `textSanitizers.accrueSanitizer` at every one
        (which carries on decoding). The position is the byte offset into the input, counted
        across chunks when a stream is decoded, and a sequence left incomplete at the end of
        the input is reported at the input's length. The skipping and substituting sanitisers
        never raise it.
    entry #position   the byte offset of the undecodable sequence
    entry #encoding   the encoding which could not decode it
  entry #encoding   the encoding this charset decodes
  entry #sanitizer   the sanitiser consulted at each undecodable byte sequence
      The `TextSanitizer` which was in scope when the charset was made. A `charsets` given
      takes it from the use site, so the same `charsets.utf8Charset` is strict in one block and
      tolerant in another according to which sanitiser is imported there; `iso88591Charset`
      alone fixes `skipSanitizer`, since every byte is valid Latin-1 and there is nothing to
      sanitise.
  entry #Self   what this charset produces: `Text`
  entry #Form   what this charset consumes: `Data`
  entry #decoded   the text of some bytes, or a stream of text from a stream of bytes
      `decoded(bytes)` decodes a whole `Data` as one `Text`. `decoded(stream)` takes a
      `Chain[Data]` and gives a `Chain[Text]`, decoding each buffer-load as it arrives and
      carrying an incomplete multi-byte sequence over to the next chunk, so the chunking of
      the input never affects the text; empty chunks are dropped from the output. Both consult
      `#sanitizer` at each fault, and so may raise `Charset.Error` through its tactic under
      `strictSanitizer` or `accrueSanitizer`. The two-parameter overload
      `decoded(bytes, omit)` is the same as `decoded(bytes)`.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textSanitizers.skipSanitizer

      val japanese = t"平ぱ記動テ使村方島"
      val bytes: Data = codepages.utf8Codepage.encoded(japanese)
      charsets.utf8Charset.decoded(bytes) == japanese
      charsets.utf8Charset.decoded(Chain(bytes)).join == japanese
      ```

entry .Codepage   encodes text as bytes in one encoding, whole or as a stream of chunks
  detail
      A `Codepage` is the encoding direction of an `Encoding` which can encode: `#encoded`
      turns `Text` into `Data`, either whole or chunk by chunk. It is made from an encoding
      with `Encoding.encoder`, found by name with `Codepage.unapply`, and most often it is a
      given imported by name from the `codepages` package — `codepages.utf8Codepage` and the
      others — which is what `in[Data]`, `write`, `Postable` givens and every other place text
      becomes bytes resolve.

      ```scala fuel
      alternative UTF-8
        code
            locally:
              import codepages.utf8Codepage
              t"café".in[Data].length   // 5: one byte each for `c`, `a` and `f`, two for `é`
      alternative UTF-16
        code
            locally:
              import codepages.utf16Codepage
              t"café".in[Data].length   // 10: a byte-order mark, then two bytes per character
      alternative UTF-16LE
        code
            locally:
              import codepages.utf16LeCodepage
              t"café".in[Data].length   // 8: the byte order is fixed, so no mark
      alternative ISO-8859-1
        code
            locally:
              import hieroglyph.codepages.iso88591Codepage
              t"café".in[Data].length   // 4: one byte per character, `é` included
      alternative ASCII
        code
            locally:
              import codepages.asciiCodepage
              t"café".in[Data].length   // 4: one byte per character, but `é` becomes `?`
      ##
      ```

      A character the encoding cannot represent is replaced (with the encoding's substitute,
      `?` for ASCII) rather than raised: `Codepage.Error` exists for a caller which wants to
      report one, but `#encoded` itself does not raise it. Encoding a stream is incremental
      and stages characters through a buffer, so a surrogate pair split across two chunks is
      encoded as one astral character, where encoding each chunk alone would corrupt it.

      A `Codepage` is `Encodable` and `Findable` with `Self = Text` and `Form = Data`, which is
      how it serves as the encoding duct in turbulence's stream stages.
  refer hieroglyph.Encoding
  refer hieroglyph.Charset
  refer hieroglyph.codepages
  entry .system   the platform's default charset as a codepage
      The encoding the JVM's `Charset.defaultCharset` names, which depends on the locale and
      `file.encoding`, so it is not a given: a program which wants the platform default asks
      for it by this name.
  entry .unapply   finds a codepage by encoding name at runtime, as an `Option`
      `Codepage.unapply(t"UTF-8")` is `Some(codepage)` if the platform has an encoding of that
      name or alias (case insensitively) which can encode, and `None` for an unknown or a
      decode-only name. Usable as an extractor in a match. This is how the `codepages` givens
      are built.
  entry .Error   a character which the encoding cannot represent
    detail
        Error 282: _character `char` cannot be encoded with the encoding `encoding`_. The
        error type for a character outside an encoding's repertoire. `Codepage#encoded` does
        not raise it — an unmappable character is replaced, as `String#getBytes` replaces it —
        so it is for callers which detect the condition themselves.
    entry #char   the character which could not be encoded
    entry #encoding   the encoding which could not encode it
  entry #encoding   the encoding this codepage encodes with, known to be able to encode
      Typed `Encoding { type CanEncode = true }`.
  entry #Self   what this codepage consumes: `Text`
  entry #Form   what this codepage produces: `Data`
  entry #encoded   the bytes of a text, or a stream of bytes from a stream of text
      `encoded(text)` encodes a whole `Text` as one `Data`, through `String#getBytes` with the
      resolved charset. `encoded(stream)` takes a `Chain[Text]` and gives a `Chain[Data]`,
      encoding buffer-load by buffer-load: a surrogate pair whose halves fall in different
      chunks is carried across and encoded whole, a stateful encoding is flushed once at the
      end, and empty chunks are dropped from the output. Malformed or unmappable input is
      replaced, never raised.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textSanitizers.skipSanitizer

      val astral = t"😀 smiles"
      val chunks: Chain[Text] = Chain(t"\ud83d", t"\ude00 smi", t"les")
      charsets.utf8Charset.decoded(codepages.utf8Codepage.encoded(chunks)).join == astral
      ```

entry .TextSanitizer   what a decoder does with a byte sequence it cannot decode
  detail
      A `TextSanitizer` is consulted by a `Charset` at every byte sequence its encoding cannot
      decode, with the position of the fault and the encoding, and answers with the character
      to write in its place, or `Unset` to write nothing. It is a contextual value chosen by
      importing one of the `textSanitizers` package's givens by name, and is captured by the
      `Charset` when that is made, so the choice is visible at the import of the code which
      decodes.

      The four provided policies: `skipSanitizer` answers `Unset`; `substituteSanitizer`
      answers `?`; `strictSanitizer` aborts with `Charset.Error` at the first fault; and
      `accrueSanitizer` raises a `Charset.Error` at each fault in a `Charset.Focus`, for a
      `validate` block to collect, then answers `?` and lets decoding continue. A custom
      policy is a lambda: `given TextSanitizer = (position, encoding) => '�'` substitutes
      the Unicode replacement character.
  refer hieroglyph.textSanitizers
  refer hieroglyph.Charset
  entry #sanitize   the character to write at a fault, or `Unset` for nothing
      Called with the byte offset of the undecodable sequence (counted across chunks when a
      stream is decoded) and the encoding. The decoder then skips the bad bytes and continues
      from the next.

entry .Measurable   how wide a character or a text is in columns of a monospaced display
  detail
      A `Measurable` gives its `Self` a width in display columns: a `Char is Measurable` says
      how many columns one character occupies, and `Measurable.general` lifts any such metric
      to `Text is Measurable` by summing. The width is read with the `metrics` extension, and
      it is what gossamer's `pad`, `center` and `fit` measure with, so a column of Japanese
      text aligns where counting characters would not.

      No metric is in scope by default: one is imported by name from the `textMetrics`
      package, since the choice is also one of cost. Under `uniformMetric` a text's width is
      its length, in constant time; under `eastAsianScriptsMetric` or
      `wideCharacterWidthMetric` every character is looked up and summed.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import textMetrics.eastAsianScriptsMetric

      'a'.metrics                   // 1
      '身'.metrics                  // 2
      t"平ぱ記動テ使村方島".metrics   // 18
      ```

      gossamer's `Grapheme` and `Writing` are also `Measurable`, through the `Char` metric in
      scope, which measures a grapheme cluster as a whole.
  refer hieroglyph.textMetrics
  refer hieroglyph.metrics
  entry .general   `Text is Measurable`, as the sum of its characters' widths under the `Char` metric in scope
      In implicit scope, so a `Char is Measurable` import is all a text measurement needs.
      The sum is over UTF-16 code units, so a metric is asked about each half of a surrogate
      pair separately.
  entry #width   the number of display columns a value occupies

entry .metrics   the width of a character or a text, through the `Measurable` in scope
    `'身'.metrics` and `t"…".metrics`: an extension on any type with a `Measurable`, which
    returns `width`. Needs a `Char is Measurable` imported by name from `textMetrics`
    (`uniformMetric`, `eastAsianScriptsMetric` or `wideCharacterWidthMetric`); a `Text` is
    measured through `Measurable.general`.

entry .textMetrics   the display-width metrics, each a given to import by name
  detail
      Which metric measures a character is decided by the `Char is Measurable` given in
      scope, and `import soundness.*` brings none of these in: one is imported by name. The
      three differ in what they count and what they cost.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      locally:
        import textMetrics.uniformMetric
        t"日本".metrics   // 2

      locally:
        import textMetrics.eastAsianScriptsMetric
        t"日本".metrics   // 4
      ```

      `uniformMetric` is right wherever the text is known to hold nothing wide, and is free;
      `eastAsianScriptsMetric` is right for terminals showing CJK text; and
      `wideCharacterWidthMetric` is the most faithful to what a terminal draws, counting
      combining marks and control characters as nothing.
  entry .uniformMetric   every character is one column
      A text's width under this metric is its length, with no lookup.
  entry .eastAsianScriptsMetric   a wide or full-width character is two columns, any other one
      Looks each character up in Unicode's `EastAsianWidth.txt` table (`Unicode.eastAsianWidth`):
      `Wide` and `FullWidth` are two columns; `Narrow`, `HalfWidth`, `Neutral`, `Ambiguous` and
      a character absent from the table are one. Combining marks still count as one here; for a
      metric which gives them nothing, use `wideCharacterWidthMetric`.
  entry .wideCharacterWidthMetric   Markus Kuhn's `wcwidth`: wide is two, combining and control is zero
      `WideCharacterWidth.width` for each character: zero for `NUL`, C0 and C1 controls,
      non-spacing and enclosing marks and format characters (so a combining accent or a
      zero-width joiner adds nothing), two for east-Asian `Wide` and `FullWidth`, and one for
      everything else. The metric gossamer's grapheme measurements are documented with, since
      a base letter with a combining mark then measures one.

entry .Unicode   Unicode's tables: character names, east-Asian widths, and a few renderings
  detail
      The `Unicode` object reads Unicode's data files from the classpath, lazily, and offers
      what they hold: `.name` and `.apply` look a character's name up either way, through
      `.unicodeData` and `.unicodeNames`; `.eastAsianWidth` gives a code point's
      `EaWidth` category, which the `textMetrics` and `WideCharacterWidth` are built on; and
      `.visible` and `.smallCaps` are two small renderings. The tables are `UnicodeData.txt`
      and `EastAsianWidth.txt` for Unicode 16.0.0, under `/hieroglyph/` on the classpath; on
      the JVM a missing table is fetched from unicode.org, and on Scala Native a missing table
      is a panic, so the resources must be embedded in the binary.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Unicode.name('é')                             // t"Latin Small Letter E With Acute"
      Unicode(t"LATIN SMALL LETTER E WITH ACUTE")   // 'é'
      Unicode.eastAsianWidth('身')                  // Unicode.EaWidth.Wide
      Unicode.visible('\t')                         // '␉'
      Unicode.smallCaps(t"hello")                   // t"ʜᴇʟʟᴏ"
      ```

      A character's name is also reached as `char.description` or `char.designation`, and a
      character by its name as `ucs"…"`, checked as the code compiles.
  refer hieroglyph.ucs
  refer hieroglyph.description
  refer hieroglyph.WideCharacterWidth
  entry .smallCaps   a text with its lowercase ASCII letters replaced by small capitals
      `a`–`z` become `ᴀ`–`ᴢ` (with `ꜰ` for `f`, `ǫ` for `q`, and `s` and `x`, which have no
      small-capital forms, left as they are); every other character is unchanged. Used by
      `communication.unicodeCharNamesCommunicable` to typeset a character's name.
  entry .EaWidth   a code point's east-Asian width category, from `EastAsianWidth.txt`
    detail
        The six categories Unicode assigns: `Neutral` (`N`), `Narrow` (`Na`), `Wide` (`W`),
        `Ambiguous` (`A`), `FullWidth` (`F`) and `HalfWidth` (`H`). `#width` reduces them to
        columns, and `.unapply` reads one from the table's two-letter code. `Ambiguous`
        characters — Greek, Cyrillic, some symbols — are wide in east-Asian legacy contexts and
        narrow elsewhere; `#width` counts them as one.
    entry .unapply   the category for a table code: `N`, `Na`, `W`, `A`, `F` or `H`
        An extractor over `Text`, `None` for any other text.
    entry #width   the category's width in columns: two for `Wide` and `FullWidth`, one otherwise
  entry .eastAsianWidth   the `EaWidth` of a character or a code point, or `Unset` if the table has none
      Takes a `Char` or an `Int` code point. The lookup is a search of the table's ranges, so
      it costs a logarithm of the table's size per character; a code point no range covers is
      `Unset`, which the `textMetrics` treat as one column.
  entry .visible   a control character as its Control Pictures glyph, for display
      `DEL` becomes `␥`, a space `␣`, and any other character at or below a space its picture
      from the `␀`–`␟` block (`'\t'` is `␉`, `'\n'` is `␊`); every other character is itself.
      For showing text whose control characters would otherwise be invisible or act on the
      terminal.
  entry .apply   the character with a Unicode name, or `Unset`
      `Unicode(t"GREEK SMALL LETTER ALPHA")` is `'α'`. The name is as `UnicodeData.txt` spells
      it, in capitals. A character beyond the Basic Multilingual Plane is a `Text` of its
      surrogate pair rather than a `Char`, so the result is `Optional[Char | Text]`. The lookup
      is `.unicodeData`, so the first call loads the table.
  entry .name   the Unicode name of a character, in title case, or `Unset`
      `Unicode.name('é')` is `t"Latin Small Letter E With Acute"`: the table's name with each
      word capitalised. `Unset` for a character the table does not name, such as a control
      character or an unassigned code point. Also reached as `char.description` and
      `char.designation`.
  entry .unicodeData   every named character, by its name as `UnicodeData.txt` spells it
      A lazily built `Map[Text, Char | Text]` from the capitalised name to the character — a
      `Char`, or a `Text` of two surrogates for a character beyond the Basic Multilingual
      Plane. Range entries such as `<CJK Ideograph, First>` are omitted.
  entry .unicodeNames   every named character's name, in title case, by the character
      The inverse of `.unicodeData`, as a `Map[Char | Text, Text]`, with each word of the name
      capitalised: `Unicode.name` reads it.
  entry .eastAsianWidths   the east-Asian width table, as ranges of code points to `EaWidth`
      A lazily loaded `TreeMap` from `hieroglyph.internal.CharRange` to `EaWidth`, with
      adjacent ranges of the same category merged. `.eastAsianWidth` searches it; it is public
      so that a tool can enumerate the table.

entry .WideCharacterWidth   Markus Kuhn's `wcwidth` algorithm: a code point's width in terminal columns
  detail
      The width a terminal gives a code point, by the rules of the classic `wcwidth`: zero for
      `NUL`, for C0 (below `0x20`) and C1 (`0x7f`–`0x9f`) controls, and for non-spacing marks,
      enclosing marks and format characters; two for a code point whose `Unicode.eastAsianWidth`
      is `Wide` or `FullWidth`; and one for everything else. It takes an `Int` code point, so
      an emoji beyond the Basic Multilingual Plane (`0x1f468`) is measured whole, as two.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      WideCharacterWidth.width('a'.toInt)     // 1
      WideCharacterWidth.width(0x0300)        // 0: a combining grave accent
      WideCharacterWidth.width('日'.toInt)    // 2
      WideCharacterWidth.width(0x1f468)       // 2: an emoji
      ```

      `textMetrics.wideCharacterWidthMetric` applies it per `Char`.
  refer hieroglyph.textMetrics
  entry .width   the terminal width of a code point: zero, one or two columns

entry .Chars   the superscript and subscript forms of digits and arithmetic signs
  detail
      Two partial functions from a character to its Unicode superscript or subscript form,
      defined for the digits `0`–`9` and for `(`, `)`, `+`, `-` and `=`. They are reached as
      `char.superscript` and `char.subscript`, which give `Unset` where no form exists.
  entry .superscript   `'2'` to `'²'`, for digits, parentheses, `+`, `-` and `=`
  entry .subscript   `'2'` to `'₂'`, for digits, parentheses, `+`, `-` and `=`

entry .superscript   a character's superscript form, or `Unset`
    An extension on `Char`: `'2'.superscript` is `'²'`, `'('.superscript` is `'⁽'`, and a
    letter — for which Unicode has no consistent superscript alphabet — is `Unset`. Defined
    for `0`–`9`, `(`, `)`, `+`, `-` and `=`, through `Chars.superscript`.

entry .subscript   a character's subscript form, or `Unset`
    An extension on `Char`: `'2'.subscript` is `'₂'`, `'+'.subscript` is `'₊'`, and any
    character other than `0`–`9`, `(`, `)`, `+`, `-` and `=` is `Unset`. Through
    `Chars.subscript`.

entry .description   a character's Unicode name, in title case, or `Unset`
    An extension on `Char`: `'é'.description` is `t"Latin Small Letter E With Acute"`, and a
    character the Unicode database does not name is `Unset`. The same as `designation`, and
    as `Unicode.name`; the first call loads the table.

entry .designation   a character's Unicode name, in title case, or `Unset`
    An extension on `Char`, identical to `description`: `'ß'.designation` is
    `t"Latin Small Letter Sharp S"`. `communication.unicodeCharNamesCommunicable` renders a character
    in a message by it.

entry .minuscule   a character in lower case
    An extension on `Char`: `Character.toLowerCase`. The predicate is `minuscular`. The pair
    is named for the letter forms rather than `lower`/`upper`, which are gossamer's `Text` case
    conversions and would collide with them in the `soundness` umbrella.

entry .majuscule   a character in upper case
    An extension on `Char`: `Character.toUpperCase`. The predicate is `majuscular`.

entry .whitespace   whether a character is whitespace
    An extension on `Char`: `Character.isWhitespace`, which counts spaces, tabs, line breaks
    and the Unicode space separators but not the no-break space.

entry .control   whether a character is an ISO control character
    An extension on `Char`: `Character.isISOControl`, which is `0x00`–`0x1f` and
    `0x7f`–`0x9f`.

entry .printable   whether a character is neither a control character nor whitespace
    An extension on `Char`: `!control && !whitespace`. A character which marks on the page.

entry .letter   whether a character is a letter in any script
    An extension on `Char`: `Character.isLetter`, so `'é'.letter` and `'身'.letter` hold as
    `'a'.letter` does, and `'7'.letter` and `'.'.letter` do not.

entry .digit   whether a character is a digit in any script
    An extension on `Char`: `Character.isDigit`, which includes the digits of other scripts
    (Arabic-Indic, Devanagari, …) as well as `0`–`9`.

entry .alphanumeric   whether a character is a letter or a digit
    An extension on `Char`: `Character.isLetterOrDigit`. Punctuation and whitespace are
    neither.

entry .majuscular   whether a character is upper case
    An extension on `Char`: `Character.isUpperCase`. `'A'.majuscular` holds; `'a'` and the
    caseless `'7'` do not. The conversion is `majuscule`.

entry .minuscular   whether a character is lower case
    An extension on `Char`: `Character.isLowerCase`. `'a'.minuscular` holds; `'A'` and the
    caseless `'7'` do not. The conversion is `minuscule`.

entry .unicode   a code point as text, as `0x1f600.unicode`
    An extension on `Int`: the `Text` of the character with that code point, which is two
    UTF-16 code units for a character beyond the Basic Multilingual Plane. An invalid code
    point throws the JDK's `IllegalArgumentException`.

entry .enc   the `enc"…"` interpolator: an encoding by name, checked as the code compiles
    `enc"UTF-8"` names a character encoding and checks, while the code compiles, that the
    platform has an encoding of that name (by display name or alias, case insensitively). A
    name it does not is a compile error, _the encoding `ABCDEF` was not available_ (SN-541),
    and a name which is not IANA-registered compiles with a warning that it may not be
    universally available. The result's `CanEncode` type member is fixed to `true` or `false`
    from the platform's table, so `enc"ISO-2022-CN".decoder` compiles and
    `enc"ISO-2022-CN".encoder` does not.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*
    import textSanitizers.skipSanitizer

    enc"UTF-8".encoder.encoded(t"café").length           // 5
    enc"ISO-8859-1".encoder.encoded(t"café").length      // 4
    enc"ISO-2022-CN".decoder.decoded(Data(0x41, 0x42))   // t"AB"
    ```

    The interpolator takes no `$`-substitutions: the name must be a literal, since it is
    checked at compile time. The compile-time check consults the compiler's JVM, so an
    encoding present there and absent at runtime fails when the encoding's `charset` is first
    resolved.

entry .ucs   the `ucs"…"` interpolator: a character by its Unicode name, checked as the code compiles
    `ucs"GREEK SMALL LETTER ALPHA"` is `'α'`, and `ucs"Grinning Face"` is the text of
    U+1F600's surrogate pair: the result is `Char | Text`, a `Char` for a character in the
    Basic Multilingual Plane and a `Text` beyond it. The name is matched against
    `UnicodeData.txt` in capitals, so case does not matter, and a name the database does not
    have is a compile error, _the unicode character `…` does not exist_ (SN-954). Takes no
    `$`-substitutions.

    ```scala
    import soundness.*

    ucs"LATIN SMALL LETTER E WITH ACUTE" == 'é'
    ```

entry .GraphemeBreak   where one user-perceived character ends and the next begins, by UAX #29
  detail
      What a reader sees as one character may be several code points — a flag is two regional
      indicators, a family emoji is several joined by zero-width joiners, an accented letter
      may be a base and a combining mark — so code which truncates, reverses or measures text
      by code point splits characters in half. `GraphemeBreak.boundaries` finds the boundaries
      between extended grapheme clusters by the rules of Unicode Standard Annex #29 (version
      16.0.0, including the Indic conjunct rule GB9c), and the property lookups behind it are
      public too.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      GraphemeBreak.boundaries(t"abc").to[List]       // List(0, 1, 2, 3)
      GraphemeBreak.boundaries(t"a\r\nb").to[List]    // List(0, 1, 3, 4): CR LF is one cluster
      GraphemeBreak.boundaries(t"🇬🇧🇫🇷").to[List]       // List(0, 4, 8): two flags
      GraphemeBreak.boundaries(t"").to[List]          // List(0)
      ```

      The tables are `GraphemeBreakProperty.txt`, `emoji-data.txt` and
      `IndicConjunctBreak.txt` from the classpath, loaded on first use. gossamer's `Writing`
      and `Grapheme` are the higher-level view: a text as its clusters.
  refer hieroglyph.Normalization
  entry .Property   the Grapheme_Cluster_Break property values
    detail
        The values UAX #29 assigns to a code point: `Other`, `Cr`, `Lf`, `Control`, `Extend`,
        `Zwj`, `RegionalIndicator`, `Prepend`, `SpacingMark`, and the Hangul jamo and syllable
        classes `L`, `V`, `T`, `Lv` and `Lvt`. `.property` reads one for a code point.
  entry .property   the Grapheme_Cluster_Break property of a code point, `Other` if the table has none
  entry .extendedPictographic   whether a code point has the Extended_Pictographic emoji property
      From `emoji-data.txt`; the property which lets an emoji sequence continue across a
      zero-width joiner.
  entry .incb   a code point's Indic_Conjunct_Break value: `0` consonant, `1` extend, `2` linker, `-1` none
      From `DerivedCoreProperties.txt`, carried on the classpath as `IndicConjunctBreak.txt`;
      the property behind rule GB9c, which keeps a consonant, virama and consonant together.
  entry .boundaries   the offsets at which grapheme clusters begin and end
      An array of UTF-16 offsets into the text, beginning with `0` and ending with the text's
      length, so a text of `n` clusters has `n + 1` boundaries and the empty text has one.
      The slice between two adjacent boundaries is one cluster.

entry .Normalization   canonical decomposition (NFD) and combining classes, from `UnicodeData.txt`
  detail
      The normalisation `CollationTable` needs: a text's canonical decomposition, with Hangul
      syllables decomposed arithmetically into their jamo and combining marks reordered by
      combining class, as Unicode Normalization Form D prescribes. Composition (NFC) is not
      provided.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*

      Normalization.decompose(t"é").to[List]    // List(0x65, 0x301): e, combining acute
      Normalization.decompose(t"각").to[List]   // List(0x1100, 0x1161, 0x11a8)
      Normalization.combiningClass(0x301)       // 230
      ```
  refer hieroglyph.CollationTable
  entry .combiningClass   a code point's canonical combining class, `0` for a starter
      Read from field 3 of `UnicodeData.txt`; values run to 240. A mark with a higher class
      sorts after one with a lower class within a run of marks.
  entry .decompose   the canonical decomposition of a text, or of an array of code points, as code points
      `decompose(text)` reads the text's code points (a surrogate pair as one) and decomposes
      them; `decompose(codepoints)` takes an array of code points. Mappings are closed
      recursively at load time, so each code point is a single table hit, and each maximal run
      of combining marks is stably sorted by combining class.

entry .CollationTable   the Unicode Collation Algorithm (UTS #10): sort keys and comparison over the DUCET
  detail
      A `CollationTable` orders text the way a dictionary does: by letters first, then by
      accents, then by case, so `cafe < café < caff` and `abc < ABC`, with `æ` between `ae`
      and `af` and unmapped CJK ideographs in code-point order. `.root` is the table built
      from the Default Unicode Collation Element Table (`allkeys.txt`, version 16.0.0), and
      `#tailor` derives a table for a language whose order differs, from a list of
      `CollationRule`s. Comparison is non-ignorable — punctuation and spaces keep their
      weights — and a sort key has the three levels.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import hieroglyph.{CollationTable, CollationRule, CollationLevel}

      val root = CollationTable.root
      root.compare(t"cafe", t"café") < 0    // true
      root.compare(t"café", t"caff") < 0    // true
      root.compare(t"abc", t"ABC") < 0      // true

      val polish = root.tailor(List(CollationRule(t"o", t"ó", CollationLevel.Primary)))
      polish.compare(t"ó", t"p") < 0        // true: ó is a letter after o
      root.compare(t"ó", t"oz") < 0         // true: in the root, ó is an accented o
      ```

      The table is not exported to the `soundness` umbrella: it is reached as
      `hieroglyph.CollationTable`, and most programs meet it through gossamer's
      `collations.unicodeCollation`, which sorts `Text` with `.root`. Loading `.root` parses
      `allkeys.txt` from the classpath (`/hieroglyph/allkeys.txt`, or unicode.org on the JVM
      if it is absent) on first use, which takes a moment; the table is then shared.
  refer hieroglyph.CollationRule
  refer hieroglyph.Normalization
  entry .root   the table built from the Default Unicode Collation Element Table
      A lazy value: built on first use from `allkeys.txt`, and shared thereafter. Panics if
      the file is on neither the classpath nor, on the JVM, reachable from unicode.org.
  entry #key   the sort key of a text, or of an array of code points
      An array of weights: every non-zero primary weight, then `0`, the secondaries, `0`, and
      the tertiaries, so that two keys compare lexicographically with `#compareKeys`. The
      input is decomposed (`Normalization.decompose`) first, contractions are matched longest
      first including the discontiguous case, and a code point with no mapping takes the
      implicit weights UTS #10 derives from its code point. For sorting many texts, compute
      each key once and compare keys.
  entry #compare   compares two texts: negative, zero or positive
      `compareKeys(key(left), key(right))`: keys both texts and compares them. Zero means the
      texts are equal at all three levels — the same letters, accents and case — which does
      not require them to be the same code points.
  entry #compareKeys   compares two sort keys lexicographically, the shorter first on a tie
  entry #tailor   a table with each rule's target sorted just after its base, at the rule's level
      Applies a list of `CollationRule`s in order, so a rule may use an earlier rule's target
      as its base (Polish `ż` after `ź` after `z`). A rule's base must have a mapping in this
      table, and at most 255 rules may share one base and level; either breach is a panic.
      This covers the common European tailoring shape and is not an interpreter for CLDR's
      full rule syntax.

entry .CollationRule   one tailoring: a target sorts immediately after a base, differing at a level
  detail
      `CollationRule(base, target, level)`: in the tailored table, `target` sorts just after
      `base`, with the difference counted at `level` — `Primary` for a distinct letter, as
      Polish `ó` is from `o`; `Secondary` for an accent; `Tertiary` for a case variant. Rules
      are applied in order by `CollationTable#tailor`. Not exported to the `soundness`
      umbrella: `hieroglyph.CollationRule`.
  refer hieroglyph.CollationTable
  refer hieroglyph.CollationLevel
  entry #base   the text, already in the table, after which the target sorts
  entry #target   the text to install
  entry #level   the level at which the target differs from its base

entry .CollationLevel   the level of a collation difference: `Primary`, `Secondary` or `Tertiary`
    `Primary` differences are between letters, `Secondary` between accents on the same
    letters, and `Tertiary` between cases. Used by `CollationRule`. Not exported to the
    `soundness` umbrella: `hieroglyph.CollationLevel`.

entry .charsets   the standard decoders, each a `Charset` given to import by name
  detail
      Which encoding bytes are read as is decided by the `Charset` given in scope, and the
      choice is never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of these in, and one is
      imported by name. Each resolves the `TextSanitizer` in scope at the use site, so the
      same `utf8Charset` is strict where `strictSanitizer` is imported and tolerant where
      `skipSanitizer` is; `iso88591Charset` alone fixes the skipping sanitiser, since every
      byte is valid Latin-1.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import charsets.utf8Charset
      import textSanitizers.strictSanitizer
      import strategies.throwUnsafely

      Data(0x63, 0x61, 0x66, 0xc3, 0xa9).read[Text]   // t"café"
      ```

      These are what turbulence's `read[Text]`, `utf8`, and the `Readable` and `Aggregable`
      givens from bytes to text resolve. An encoding not listed here is `enc"…".decoder`,
      bound as a local `given Charset`.
  refer hieroglyph.Charset
  refer hieroglyph.textSanitizers
  entry .utf8Charset   UTF-8, with the sanitiser in scope
  entry .utf16Charset   UTF-16, reading a byte-order mark and defaulting to big-endian, with the sanitiser in scope
  entry .utf16LeCharset   UTF-16 little-endian, with the sanitiser in scope
  entry .utf16BeCharset   UTF-16 big-endian, with the sanitiser in scope
  entry .asciiCharset   US-ASCII, with the sanitiser in scope
      Any byte above `0x7f` is a fault, handled as the sanitiser says.
  entry .iso88591Charset   ISO-8859-1 (Latin-1), always with the skipping sanitiser
      Every byte is a valid Latin-1 character, so there is nothing to sanitise and no
      `TextSanitizer` is needed in scope. Declared in `hieroglyph.charsets` only; the
      `soundness` umbrella's `charsets` package does not re-export it, so it is imported as
      `hieroglyph.charsets.iso88591Charset`.

entry .codepages   the standard encoders, each a `Codepage` given to import by name
  detail
      Which encoding text is written in is decided by the `Codepage` given in scope, and the
      choice is never implicit: `import soundness.*` brings none of these in, and one is
      imported by name. These are what `in[Data]`, `write`, the `Postable` and `Servable`
      givens for text formats, and every other place text becomes bytes resolve.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import codepages.utf8Codepage

      t"café".in[Data].length   // 5
      ```

      An encoding not listed here is `enc"…".encoder`, bound as a local `given Codepage`.
  refer hieroglyph.Codepage
  entry .utf8Codepage   UTF-8
  entry .utf16Codepage   UTF-16, written big-endian with a byte-order mark
  entry .utf16LeCodepage   UTF-16 little-endian, without a byte-order mark
  entry .utf16BeCodepage   UTF-16 big-endian, without a byte-order mark
  entry .asciiCodepage   US-ASCII; a character above `0x7f` is written as `?`
  entry .iso88591Codepage   ISO-8859-1 (Latin-1); a character above `0xff` is written as `?`
      Declared in `hieroglyph.codepages` only; the `soundness` umbrella's `codepages` package
      does not re-export it, so it is imported as `hieroglyph.codepages.iso88591Codepage`.

entry .textSanitizers   the policies for undecodable bytes, each a `TextSanitizer` given to import by name
  detail
      A `Charset` consults the `TextSanitizer` in scope at each byte sequence its encoding
      cannot decode, and `import soundness.*` brings none of these in: the code which decodes
      says which it wants, at its import. Strictness suits input which should be trusted
      absolutely; tolerance suits text recovered from a lossy source; accrual gives both the
      text and a list of where it was wrong.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import strategies.throwUnsafely
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics

      val badUtf8 = Data(45, -62, 49, 48)
      ```

      ```scala fuel
      alternative Skip
        code
            locally:
              import textSanitizers.skipSanitizer
              charsets.utf8Charset.decoded(badUtf8)   // t"-10"
      alternative Substitute
        code
            locally:
              import textSanitizers.substituteSanitizer
              charsets.utf8Charset.decoded(badUtf8)   // t"-?10"
      alternative Strict
        code
            locally:
              import textSanitizers.strictSanitizer
              capture[Charset.Error](charsets.utf8Charset.decoded(badUtf8))   // Charset.Error(1, enc"UTF-8")
      ##
      ```
  refer hieroglyph.TextSanitizer
  refer hieroglyph.Charset
  entry .skipSanitizer   drops an undecodable sequence and carries on
      Answers `Unset` at every fault: the text is whatever could be decoded, with no sign of
      what was lost.
  entry .substituteSanitizer   writes `?` in place of an undecodable sequence and carries on
  entry .strictSanitizer   aborts with `Charset.Error` at the first undecodable sequence
      Needs a `Tactic[Charset.Error]` in scope where it is resolved — `strategies.throwUnsafely`,
      a `safely`, `capture` or `recover` block — and the error names the byte offset of the
      fault and the encoding. The sanitiser retains the tactic, so it lives as long as the
      scope which resolved it.
  entry .accrueSanitizer   records every undecodable sequence, writes `?` and carries on
      Raises a `Charset.Error` at each fault, in focus of a `Charset.Focus(position)`, and
      then substitutes `?` and continues, so decoding completes and every fault is reported.
      Needs a `Tactic[Charset.Error]` and a `Foci[Charset.Focus]`, which a
      `validate[Charset.Focus](accumulator)` block provides: its first block says how each
      error joins the accumulator (with `prior` the focus) and its `protect` block does the
      decoding.

      ```scala
      import soundness.*
      import errorDiagnostics.stackTracesDiagnostics
      import dysasymptotics.linearSize

      case class DecodeIssues(items: List[(Int, Charset.Error)] = Nil)(using Diagnostics)
      extends Error(m"${items.size} decoding issues"):
        def +(position: Int, error: Charset.Error): DecodeIssues =
          DecodeIssues(items :+ (position, error))

      validate[Charset.Focus](DecodeIssues()):
        case error: Charset.Error => accrual + (prior.let(_.position).or(0), error)
      . protect:
          import textSanitizers.accrueSanitizer
          charsets.utf8Charset.decoded(Data(45, -62, 49, -62, 48))   // t"-?1?0", two issues
      ```

entry .communication   a `Char is Communicable` which names characters, a given to import by name
  detail
      How a `Char` is rendered when interpolated into an `m"…"` message is a choice, and this
      package's given makes it the character's Unicode name. It merges with the umbrella's
      `communication` package, and is imported by name:
      `import communication.unicodeCharNamesCommunicable`.
  entry .unicodeCharNamesCommunicable   renders a character in a message by its Unicode name, in small capitals
      `m"found $char"` with `'é'` reads _found ʟᴀᴛɪɴ sᴍᴀʟʟ ʟᴇᴛᴛᴇʀ ᴇ ᴡɪᴛʜ ᴀᴄᴜᴛᴇ [é]_: the name
      from `designation`, lower-cased and set in small capitals by `Unicode.smallCaps`,
      followed by the character itself in brackets if it is printable. A character with no
      name reads as _unknown_.

entry .Nul   the `NUL` character, `'\u0000'`, as a literal-typed constant
    Each of the eleven constants `Nul`, `Bel`, `Bs`, `Ht`, `Lf`, `Ff`, `Cr`, `Esc`, `Sqt`,
    `Dqt` and `Bsl` is a `final val` of the character's singleton type, so it may be used
    where a literal type is wanted and reads as a name where `'\u001b'` would not.

entry .Bel   the bell character, `'\u0007'`
entry .Bs   the backspace character, `'\u0008'`
entry .Ht   the horizontal tab, `'\u0009'`
entry .Lf   the line feed, `'\u000a'`
entry .Ff   the form feed, `'\u000c'`
entry .Cr   the carriage return, `'\u000d'`
entry .Esc   the escape character, `'\u001b'`, which begins a terminal control sequence
entry .Sqt   the single quote, `'\''`
entry .Dqt   the double quote, `'"'`
entry .Bsl   the backslash, `'\\'`

entry .internal   the macros behind `enc"…"` and `ucs"…"`, and the `CharRange` key of the width table
  hidden
  detail
      Not for direct use: the object exists so that the macros have a stable home, and so
      that `Unicode.eastAsianWidths` has a compact key type.
  entry .CharRange   an inclusive range of code points, packed into a `Long`
    detail
        `CharRange(from, to)`, or `CharRange(char)` for a range of one, packed as
        `(from << 32) + to` so that ranges order by their first code point. The key of
        `Unicode.eastAsianWidths`.
    entry .apply   a range from two code points, or of one character or code point
    entry .textualizable   renders a range as `from..to`
    entry .comparable   orders ranges as the `Long`s they are: by first code point
  entry .from   the first code point of a `CharRange`
  entry .to   the last code point of a `CharRange`
  entry .contains   whether a `CharRange` includes a character
  entry .encoding   the macro behind `enc"…"`: checks the name against the platform's encodings
      Halts with SN-541 for a name the platform lacks, warns for one not registered with
      IANA, and splices a lookup whose `CanEncode` type is fixed from the table.
  entry .char   the macro behind `ucs"…"`: a character by its Unicode name
      Halts with SN-954 for a name `UnicodeData.txt` does not have.
